<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vigraharaja IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1150–<a href="/years/1164">1164</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Visaladeva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vigraharaja IV was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chahamanas</a> of Shakambhari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in the mid-12th century who expanded the kingdom north by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> from the Tomara dynasty, whose chiefs continued as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. A patron of learning, he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play Harakeli Nataka, parts of which were inscribed on stone slabs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> in a building later converted into the Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra mosque.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vigraharaja-iv.jpg" alt="Vigraharaja IV" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vigraha_Raja_IV_of_the_Chauhans_of_Ajmer_Circa_1150-1164.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
